[Music]  we're just over a week away from  thanksgiving can you believe it and many  families are looking for a  safe way to celebrate as coronavirus  cases soar the cdc  recommends only celebrating with people  in your household  but if you are joining others the cdc  says you should wear a mask  when you're not eating stay six feet  apart and if you're inside  keep the windows open psychologist and  cbs news contributor lisa demour joins  us with how to handle  difficult decisions and conversations  around the holidays lisa good morning  thanks for being with us  what is what is your advice to people  about it good to see you yeah great to  see you too  i wish you could be here many people are  still deciding how to  to deal with the holiday as close as it  is what's your advice  you know this is such a tough one and  it's hard because there's really no good  decision you know either people have to  deal with disappointing themselves or  their families or they have to assume  some really frightening risks  so the first thing i think to do is to  recognize that there's no  perfect way to go about this and then to  make the choice that actually  one can really live with and the  challenge we're up against right now  is that we have been in this pandemic so  long and it feels tedious and familiar  and yet we have to reconcile that with  the fact that it is as dangerous as it  has  ever been and i think we really need to  let those record rates drive decision  making  right once you make a decision about  what you're going to do you have to have  the conversation with the family  what's the best way to do that  you know i think different families  handle this in different ways you know  some families do well by getting this  information in doses  right so it might be worth calling and  saying i'm having questions about coming  and then following up later and saying  i've decided i can't come  other families do well getting hard news  all at once and so that may be the way  to go  what we want to bear in mind is that  when it comes to delivering bad news  what people like least is surprises so  if you've decided that you're not going  to go and you know that's going to be  hard for someone to hear  you should probably tell them as soon as  you've made that decision yourself  what about kids lisa this has been such  a hard year for them a lot of them  obviously looking forward to holidays  and traditions  that they've had for years what do you  how do you approach kids  about this  you know with kids i think we start with  safety they understand that you know we  say we have to make the decision that is  safe  and then we are clear and calm and  direct  about telling them what's happening and  i think if we  cannot do the holidays as we normally do  we make room for them to be sad  and disappointed we don't have to talk  them out of it they have a right to  those feelings  after that i think we can work with them  about getting creative and kids are  great at this  so we could say maybe look we're not  going to do our regular thanksgiving but  maybe we do you know  a movie marathon and four kinds of  popcorn if they're still struggling  and they might be what we can say is  that there are times when we make  personal sacrifices  on behalf of a greater good and that's  what we're doing here  and we can actually help them feel proud  about their part in that  yeah that they're playing a role you  know joe biden has mentioned this about  the empty chair at the table it's always  tough that first holiday  after you've lost someone what's your  advice to families that are going  through that that's a lot of families  this year  that is a lot of families there's a lot  of people in that boat so the first  thing i would say is we should be  mindful of who those people are in our  communities we should be reaching out to  them  if there's a safe way to connect in  person consider that otherwise just  reach out digitally  i would also say that people who are  suffering should not hesitate to reach  out there is help available  but the piece that we want to remember  and the story about that chair really  gets at that  is that holidays are about times of  reflection  and gratitude and thinking about and  connecting with the people we love  and just because we can't do that in the  regular way doesn't mean that we can't  do it  this year lisa i want to pick up on  connecting with people you love because  if those people come over and your  household tries to follow the cdc  guidelines windows open six feet apart  you wear a mask when you're not eating  there are gonna be families split over  the wisdom  of those recommendations maybe arguing  about it if you're the host  how do you get in the middle say your  daughter arguing with your uncle  over whether to wear a mask  you know i think we say look i love you  both we all want to get together and  enjoy each other and have a good time  and in order for that to be possible  people have to be comfortable  and i know we don't want to do anything  that makes someone here uncomfortable so  let's just go ahead and follow the cdc  guidelines  that way we can all be at ease all right  going to be an interesting thanksgiving  blame it on the cdc yeah lisa demour  thank you so much for being with us  neutral third party  yeah okay

